course: myths
lesson: 31
title: Friends who cling to a legend
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Arguments about facts are rarely only about facts. When Maisie says "I'm not talking to you until you admit goldfish are stupid", the fight is about winning now, not about fish.
explain: A good checker keeps two things apart: what is true, and how the other person feels. You can be right about the fish and still lose a friend by shouting "WRONG!" in front of the whole class.
explain: So check before you argue, check together rather than against each other, and never make it about someone's family, cleverness or feelings.
explain: Ethan got upset when a friend shouted that he was wrong about lightning in front of everyone; Kofi, whose dad is a lorry driver, felt that calling tomatoes fruit meant calling his dad a liar; and Amelia keeps a score of who knows more facts.
explain: Sometimes the wise words are "you were right", sometimes "you're both sort of right", and sometimes "it doesn't matter, want to play?"
fact: Ostriches lay the biggest eggs of any living bird.
fact: In a thunderstorm a car protects you mainly because of its metal body, not because of its rubber tyres.
fact: Goldfish can remember things for months; they can be trained to come for food at a signal.
fact: Botanists (plant scientists) call tomatoes fruits because they hold seeds, but cooks treat them as vegetables, and in 1893 a US court case called them vegetables for tax.
fact: Peanuts are not true nuts; they are legumes, like peas and beans, and their pods grow underground.
fact: Bats are not blind; they can see, and many also use echolocation (finding things by listening to the echoes of their own squeaks).
fact: The Sahara is the largest hot desert; the largest desert of all is Antarctica, because a desert is a place with very little rain or snow.
fact: Since 2006 astronomers have called Pluto a dwarf planet, though some scientists still argue about the definition.
fact: Lemmings (small furry animals of the far north) do not jump off cliffs to die all together; a 1958 nature film helped spread the story with scenes that were staged.
fact: Correcting someone in front of everyone often hurts more than the mistake itself.
fact: Betting things on facts can lose you both the thing and the friend; checking together costs nothing.
fact: Saying sorry after a row matters more than who was right.
exercise: Which living bird lays the biggest eggs? | The ostrich | The emu | The eagle | The penguin
exercise: Charlotte said ostriches lay the biggest eggs, and her friend said she was wrong. What should the friend say now? | Sorry, you were right; I should have checked first | You're still wrong, emus beat them | It doesn't matter, eggs are boring | You only got it right by luck
exercise: Why did Ethan get upset with his friend? | The friend corrected him rudely in front of everyone | The friend got all the facts about lightning wrong too | The friend refused to talk about lightning | The friend agreed with him too quickly
exercise: What mainly protects you in a car in a thunderstorm? | Its metal body | Its rubber tyres | Its glass windows | Its seat belts
exercise: How long can goldfish remember things? | For months | For three seconds | For three minutes | Only until they swim round the bowl
exercise: Maisie won't talk to you until you admit goldfish are stupid. What's a good move? | Suggest testing it together with the class fish | Admit it to end the row, even though it's false | Stop talking to her forever | Tell everyone Maisie believes a myth
exercise: Are tomatoes fruit or vegetables? | Fruit to botanists, vegetables to cooks | Only vegetables, scientists agree | Only fruit, cooks are wrong | Neither, they are actually a kind of nut
exercise: Kofi asks if you're calling his dad a liar about tomatoes. What's the kind and true reply? | No, cooks call them veg, scientists call them fruit | Yes, your dad doesn't know anything | Lorry drivers can't know about food | Your dad is right, and all the scientists are wrong
exercise: What are peanuts really? | Legumes, like peas and beans | Tree nuts, like walnuts and almonds | Seeds that fall from a palm tree | Roots, like carrots and parsnips
exercise: Isaac bets his Pokémon card that bats are blind. What's the friendly move? | Say "let's not bet, let's just check" | Take the bet, because you'll win his card | Bet your own card that bats can't fly | Tell him he's stupid for believing it
exercise: Can bats see? | Yes, and many also use echolocation | No, they are completely blind from birth | Only in very bright daylight, not at night | Only the baby bats can see, not the adults
exercise: What is the largest desert in the world? | Antarctica | The Sahara | The Gobi | The Arabian Desert
exercise: Grace is in tears because everyone laughed when she said the Sahara is the biggest desert. What's kind and true? | You were nearly right, it's the biggest hot desert | They were right to laugh, the Sahara is really tiny | The Sahara isn't a desert at all | Don't cry, deserts don't matter
exercise: Jake says "let's agree to disagree about Pluto". Is that OK? | Yes, it's partly about a definition people still argue over | No, someone must win every argument | No, Pluto is definitely a full planet | Yes, because facts don't exist
exercise: Do lemmings jump off cliffs to die all together? | No, a 1958 film helped spread the staged story | Yes, they do it every single year up in Norway | Yes, but only the very old and sick ones jump off | No, because lemmings can fly away from the cliff
exercise: Finn says sorry for calling you a know-it-all. What's the best answer? | Of course, I'm sorry I went on about it too | Only if you admit I was right | No, I'll never forgive you | Fine, but you're still wrong about the sharks thing
exercise: Amelia says you're jealous because she knows more facts. What's a good idea? | A fact swap where each brings a true fact | A fact battle where the loser has to say sorry | Stop telling her any facts at all | Say she knows nothing, so you're even
test: "ur WRONG goldfish forget everything in 3 seconds everyone knows that" What's true, said kindly? | They can actually remember for months; want to look it up? | You're right, three seconds exactly | They forget in one second, not three | Shut up, goldfish don't have brains
test: Your friend was right about ostriches, but you argued and she walked off. What fixes things best? | Saying sorry and that she was right | Finding a different fact to win the next row on | Waiting for her to say sorry to you first | Telling the whole class that she was rude
test: You know the lightning fact and Ethan doesn't. What's the best way to share it? | Quietly, and offer to look it up together | Shout "WRONG!" so everyone hears | Laugh and tell the teacher | Keep correcting him until he finally admits it
test: Ruby insists peanuts are nuts because of the name. What's a fun way to settle it? | Show her they grow underground like beans | Bet her lunch on it | Tell her names are always lies | Say it doesn't matter at all and just walk off
test: What would happen if you made every fact argument a bet? | You'd lose things and maybe friends, even when right | You'd become the cleverest in school | Everyone would want to argue with you | Facts would become more true
test: "omg everyone laughed at grace for saying sahara lol" What should a good friend do? | Stand by Grace and point out it's the biggest hot desert | Laugh too, so you're not left out | Tell Grace she should've known | Say nothing and change the subject
test: Sophie says it doesn't matter whether lemmings jump off cliffs. What's wise? | Agree to drop it; it doesn't need a fight | Keep going until she admits it | Tell her she's lazy for not caring | Make her sit and watch a whole film about lemmings
test: Kofi gets cross: "My dad says tomatoes are veg!" What should you NOT do? | Say his dad doesn't know anything | Say cooks really do call them veg | Say scientists call them fruit | Say you're both sort of right
test: In a cartoon, a bat bumps into everything because "bats are blind as a bat". What's the correction? | Bats can see, and many use echolocation too | Bats really are totally blind | Bats can only see in colour, and only at night | Bats use their noses to see
test: Two friends argue whether Pluto is a planet. Which is true? | Astronomers call it a dwarf planet, though some still debate it | Everyone agrees it's the biggest planet | Pluto was never discovered | Pluto is a moon of Earth
test: Amelia counts who knows more facts. What does she forget? | Facts are for sharing, not scoring | Facts are only really meant for teachers | Facts stop being true once they are shared | Only grown-ups are able to know real facts
exam: Identify the bird species, among those presently living, which produces the largest egg. | The ostrich | The emu | The albatross | The golden eagle
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the botanical classification of the tomato. | It is a fruit, though commonly treated as a vegetable in cookery | It is a vegetable in every scientific sense | It is a variety of nut | It is a root, in the manner of the carrot
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the capacity of bats in respect of vision? | Bats possess sight, frequently supplemented by echolocation | Bats are entirely devoid of sight | Bats perceive solely by smell | Bats are able to see only in total darkness
exam: Determine the desert of greatest area, given that a desert is defined by scarcity of precipitation. | Antarctica | The Sahara | The Kalahari | The Gobi
